                                        "entry_id": "c85bda74-60eb-348d-a3d9-bdb6fe5c7beb",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The consumption of living plant tissue by animals."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1eb8260c-1fea-4602-a306-85452ef2f510",
                                                "sense_id": "1a8048f6-0e27-324f-8ba7-ee4cc9970e76",
                                                "text": "Herbivory is a paradoxical interspecific interaction. On the one hand, upward of a quarter of all species are herbivorous insects (Strong et al., 1984) and some additional fraction of the noninsect species is also herbivorous, so plant-herbivore interactions are common. On the other hand, the simple observation is that, to a first approximation, the earth is green (Hairston et al., 1960), despite the combined effect of those herbivores."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
